WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.230
Open the Synthetic Reporter Entry and
select its DNA block.

2
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.750
This bundled example contains seven hundred
twenty bases.

3
00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.750
In the Inspector, click Translate.

4
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.230
The panel lists the six possible reading
frames: three in the forward

5
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.500
direction and three on the reverse
complement.

6
00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:25.510
Compare the frame labels before
interpreting a peptide.

7
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.150
Next, switch the DNA block to Detail.

8
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.195
Turn on its inline translation track.

9
00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:36.950
Amino-acid letters now align with the DNA,
alongside the sequence coordinates

10
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.350
and feature annotations. This view helps
you relate a protein position

11
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:46.060
to the underlying bases. Scroll to the
existing Synthetic Reporter Protein

12
00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:51.290
block and select it. The Inspector changes
to Codons and shows a

13
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.640
Back-translation preview marked Draft.
Review the target host and codon

14
00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:58.590
strategy before using this output.

15
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:03.070
Here the preview contains seven hundred
seventeen DNA bases.

16
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.930
Back-translation proposes one possible
coding sequence; it does not recover

17
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.109
a unique original DNA sequence.
